Given, \[V_C(0^-)=V_0=10~\text{V},\qquad i_L(0^-)=0\] Switch is closed at \(t=0\). <img src="img/7e6c80ed69d58861.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Assuming diode is forward biased, KVL gives \[\frac{V_0}{s}-sL_0I(s)-\frac{I(s)}{C_0s}=0\] \[I(s)\!\left(sL_0+\frac{1}{C_0s}\right)=\frac{V_0}{s}\] \[I(s)=\frac{V_0C_0}{L_0C_0s^2+1}
=\frac{V_0}{L_0\!\left(s^2+\frac{1}{L_0C_0}\right)}
\tag{i}\] The capacitor voltage is \[V_C(s)=\frac{V_0}{s}-\frac{I(s)}{C_0s}
=\frac{V_0}{s}
-\frac{V_0}{L_0C_0}
\left[\frac{1}{s\left(s^2+\frac{1}{L_0C_0}\right)}\right]
\tag{ii}\] Using \[\frac{1}{s\left(s^2+\frac{1}{L_0C_0}\right)}
=
\frac{L_0C_0}{s}
-\frac{sL_0C_0}{s^2+\frac{1}{L_0C_0}},\] equation (ii) becomes \[V_C(s)=\frac{V_0s}{s^2+\frac{1}{L_0C_0}}
=
V_0\!\left[\frac{s}
{s^2+\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{L_0C_0}}\right)^2}\right].\] Hence, \[V_C(t)
=
V_0\cos\!\left(\frac{t}{\sqrt{L_0C_0}}\right)
=
V_0\cos(\omega_0 t),
\qquad
\omega_0=\frac{1}{\sqrt{L_0C_0}}.\] Since the diode conducts only during the positive half cycle, <img src="img/01d858243f328494.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> \[\omega_0 t=\pi
\;\Rightarrow\;
t=\pi\sqrt{L_0C_0}
=\pi\sqrt{(1\times10^{-3})(10\times10^{-6})}
=0.1\pi~\text{ms}.\] Therefore, \[V_C=V_0\cos\pi=-V_0=-10~\text{V}.\] The diode now becomes reverse biased. <img src="img/0888938ad44862ff.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Since there is no discharge path, the capacitor continues to hold \(-10\) V. As \[0.5~\text{s}\gg0.1\pi~\text{ms},\] \[V_C(0.5~\text{s})=-10~\text{V}.\] \[\boxed{-10.0}\]

