For a voltage follower, the feedback factor is \[\beta = 1.\] The closed-loop output impedance is given by \[Z_{out(CL)}=\frac{Z_{out}}{1+A\beta}.\] Given, \[A=10^{4}, \qquad Z_{out}=100\,\Omega.\] Hence, \[Z_{out(CL)}
=\frac{100}{1+10^{4}}
=\frac{100}{10001}
\approx 0.009999\,\Omega.\] Converting into milliohms, \[0.009999\,\Omega
=9.999\,\text{m}\Omega
\approx 10.0\,\text{m}\Omega.\] \[\boxed{10.0}\]

The circuit is an inverting amplifier with a parallel RC feedback network. <img src="img/805ba746aeb4f94b.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> The feedback impedance is \[Z_{f}=R_{f}\parallel\frac{1}{j\omega C}
=\frac{R_{f}}{1+j\omega R_{f}C}.\] Hence, the closed-loop gain is \[\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}
=-\frac{Z_{f}}{R_{in}}
=-\frac{R_{f}}{R_{in}\left(1+j\omega R_{f}C\right)}.\] The magnitude of the gain is \[\left|\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}\right|
=\frac{R_{f}/R_{in}}
{\sqrt{1+\left(\omega R_{f}C\right)^2}}.\] For a gain of \(0\,\text{dB}\), \[\left|\frac{V_{out}}{V_{in}}\right|=1.\] Substituting \(R_{f}=100\,\text{k}\Omega\), \(R_{in}=10\,\text{k}\Omega\), and \(C=1\,\text{nF}\), \[\frac{10}{\sqrt{1+\left(\omega\times100\times10^{3}\times10^{-9}\right)^2}}=1.\] Therefore, \[1+\left(\omega\times10^{-4}\right)^2=100,\] \[\omega=\frac{\sqrt{99}}{10^{-4}}
=9.9499\times10^{4}\,\text{rad/s}.\] Hence, \[f=\frac{\omega}{2\pi}
=\frac{9.9499\times10^{4}}{2\pi}
\approx15.84\,\text{kHz}.\] \[\boxed{15.84}\]

Since the emitter is grounded, \[V_{E}=0,\qquad
V_{CE}=5.2\,\text{V}\] Hence, \[V_{C}=5.2\,\text{V},\qquad
V_{B}=V_{BE}=0.7\,\text{V}\] The base current through \(R_{1}\) is \[I_{B}=\frac{V_{C}-V_{B}}{R_{1}}
=\frac{5.2-0.7}{100\,\text{k}\Omega}
=45\,\mu\text{A}\] Current through \(R_{2}\) is \[I_{R_{2}}=\frac{12-5.2}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
=6.8\,\text{mA}\] Applying KCL at the collector node, \[I_{R_{2}}=I_{C}+I_{B}\] \[I_{C}=6.8-0.045=6.755\,\text{mA}\] Therefore, \[\beta=\frac{I_{C}}{I_{B}}
=\frac{6.755\,\text{mA}}{45\,\mu\text{A}}
=150.11\] \[\boxed{150.11}\] <img src="img/eda02ac45969a772.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)">

The plant is \(P(s)=\dfrac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}\) The PID controller is \(C(s)=K_P+\dfrac{K_I}{s}+K_Ds\) Since the controller contains an integral term, the open-loop system is of Type-1. For a Type-1 system, the steady-state error for a unit ramp input is \[e_{ss}=\frac{1}{K_v}\] where \(K_v=\lim\limits_{s\to0}sC(s)P(s)\) \[K_v=\lim_{s\to0}s\left(K_P+\frac{K_I}{s}+K_Ds\right)\frac{1}{(s+1)(s+2)}
=\lim_{s\to0}\frac{K_Ps+K_I+K_Ds^2}{(s+1)(s+2)}
=\frac{K_I}{2}\] Thus, the velocity error constant depends only on the integral gain \(K_I\) After changing the controller gains, \(K_P'=2K_P,\) \(K_I'=3K_I,\) \(K_D'=K_D.\) Therefore, \[K_v'=\frac{3K_I}{2}=3K_v\] Hence, the new steady-state error becomes \[e_{ss}'
=\frac{1}{K_v'}
=\frac{1}{3K_v}
=\frac{e_{ss}}{3}\] Therefore, the steady-state error decreases by a factor of \(3\) \[\boxed{(C)\;\text{Decreases by a factor of 3}}\]
